description:
xtask history's stale-invocation cleanup sweep can send SIGTERM/SIGKILL to an unrelated live process due to PID reuse, with no secondary identity check.

xtask/src/history/db/background.rs (stale-row detection) + xtask/src/history/db/process.rs:36-47 (`try_reap_zombie_pid`):
```rust
if let Some(pid) = candidate.pid {
    try_reap_zombie_pid(pid);
    reaped_zombies += 1;
}
```
```rust
let _ = nix::sys::signal::kill(nix_pid, SIGTERM);
...
if nix::sys::signal::kill(nix_pid, None).is_ok() {
    let _ = nix::sys::signal::kill(nix_pid, SIGKILL);
}
```
A stale invocation row stores PID X from a long-finished xtask process. If the OS has since reassigned PID X to a completely unrelated live process (a common occurrence after enough process churn), the open-time sweep classifies it as "alive+stale" purely from the PID number and sends SIGTERM then SIGKILL to that unrelated process. There is no secondary identity check (process command line, process group, start time correlation) before signaling.

This is a real safety bug in xtask's own tooling: an operator running any xtask command that triggers this sweep could have an unrelated process on their machine killed.

Found via a narrate-through audit of xtask/src/history/db/{audit,background,exercise,git,process,prediction,sandbox_meta}.rs using gpt-5.3-codex-spark (2026-08-11).

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Create a stale invocation row referencing a PID that has since been reused by an unrelated live process (simulate by recording a PID, letting that process exit, then starting a new unrelated process and hoping/forcing it to reuse the PID, or by directly calling try_reap_zombie_pid with a live unrelated PID).
2. Trigger the stale-invocation cleanup sweep (runs at xtask startup / on relevant commands).
3. Observe the unrelated process receives SIGTERM then SIGKILL with no verification it's actually the original xtask-spawned process.

