description:
sinexctl ops replay watch's JSON/NDJSON/Dot/Yaml output paths never detect ReplayState::Failed, unlike the Table path which explicitly checks it and returns Err.

crate/sinexctl/src/commands/replay.rs:537-589 (execute_watch):
```rust
OutputFormat::Json | OutputFormat::Ndjson | OutputFormat::Dot => loop {
    let op = client.replay_status(operation_id).await?;
    println!("{}", format_json(&op)?);
    if op.state.is_terminal() { break; }
    sleep(Duration::from_secs(interval)).await;
},
OutputFormat::Yaml => {
    let op = client.replay_status(operation_id).await?;
    println!("{}", format_yaml(&op)?);
}
```
ReplayState::is_terminal() (crate/sinex-primitives/src/rpc/replay.rs:122-127) is Completed|Failed|Cancelled. The JSON/Ndjson/Dot branch breaks the loop on ANY terminal state -- including Failed -- then falls through to Ok(()). The Yaml branch doesn't even poll (single status fetch, despite the subcommand being documented "Watch replay operation progress in real-time", line 185) and also ignores state entirely.

Two call sites affected: standalone `sinexctl ops replay watch --operation-id X` (line 471) and the auto-watch after a non-dry-run `sinexctl ops replay run ...` (line 678, execute_watch(...).await? then execute_run returns Ok(())). This is a mutating, potentially destructive operation (event replay/cascade). A CI/cron script running `sinexctl ops replay run --source X --format json` gets exit code 0 even when the replay genuinely failed server-side -- only a caller parsing the JSON body's terminal state/error_details (not the overwhelmingly common exit-code check) would catch it. The machine-readable format is broken while the human Table format was correctly fixed -- a real asymmetry.

Found via a targeted follow-up audit (Sonnet) hunting for the success-reported-on-failure pattern across sinexctl/xtask (2026-08-11).

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Trigger a replay operation that will fail server-side.
2. Run sinexctl ops replay watch --operation-id X --format json (or run a non-dry-run ops replay run with --format json, which auto-watches).
3. Observe exit code 0 despite the replay having failed -- contrast with --format table, which correctly detects Failed and returns a non-zero exit.

journald.rs and systemd.rs both fabricate ts_orig on a missing/malformed __REALTIME_TIMESTAMP field and tag the fallback TimingEvidence::Intrinsic, which makes admission trust and persist it verbatim -- bypassing the raw.temporal_ledger-based resolution material events are designed to use. Same bug shape as the already-fixed-in-progress activitywatch.rs finding (sinex-dmz9), same root mechanism: a Timestamp::now() fallback value gets tagged with a TimingEvidence variant that resolved_quality() treats as "resolved" (Intrinsic/InferredMtime/RealtimeCapture/UserDeclared), which makes intent_to_event_with_anchor (crate/sinexd/src/runtime/parser/adapter_source.rs:2623-2626) call .at_time_with_quality(...) and permanently skip admission's raw.temporal_ledger resolution (crate/sinexd/src/event_engine/jetstream_consumer/prepare.rs:171, resolve_ready_ts_orig).

crate/sinexd/src/sources/source_contracts/system/journald.rs:174-185, :276-280:
```rust
let timestamp = if timestamp_us > 0 {
    Timestamp::from_unix_timestamp_nanos(i128::from(timestamp_us) * 1_000).unwrap_or_else(Timestamp::now)
} else {
    Timestamp::now()
};
...
.ts_orig(timestamp)
.timing(TimingEvidence::Intrinsic { field: "__REALTIME_TIMESTAMP".into(), confidence: TimingConfidence::Intrinsic })
```

crate/sinexd/src/sources/source_contracts/system/systemd.rs:134-144, :268-272 -- identical shape, same journal field:
```rust
let timestamp = if timestamp_us > 0 {
    Timestamp::from_unix_timestamp(timestamp_us / 1_000_000).unwrap_or_else(Timestamp::now)
} else {
    Timestamp::now()
};
...
.timing(TimingEvidence::Intrinsic { field: "__REALTIME_TIMESTAMP".into(), confidence: TimingConfidence::Intrinsic })
```

Failure scenario: a journal entry with a missing, zero, or nanosecond-overflow __REALTIME_TIMESTAMP silently gets ts_orig=parse-time-now(), tagged as if it were a real intrinsic source timestamp. On replay the parser re-runs and calls now() again at a different wall-clock time -- non-reproducible, wrong temporal ordering.

Found via a targeted follow-up audit (Sonnet) of this session's confirmed activitywatch.rs finding, cross-referencing every Material-provenance parser for the same TimingEvidence-mistagging shape (2026-08-11).

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Feed a journal entry with a missing/zero/overflow-triggering __REALTIME_TIMESTAMP field to journald.rs's or systemd.rs's row parser.
2. Observe the fallback Timestamp::now() gets tagged TimingEvidence::Intrinsic (a resolved-quality tag) rather than TimingEvidence::Atemporal.
3. Trace intent_to_event_with_anchor (adapter_source.rs:2623-2626) -- confirm resolved_quality() returns Some for Intrinsic, so .at_time_with_quality() is called and admission's raw.temporal_ledger resolution never runs.

Two issues in sinex-primitives' core JSON-sanitization validator (crate/sinex-primitives/src/validation/core.rs), used at the redact_batch/persistence chokepoint:

1. Lines 320-327 -- NUL-character stripping on JSON object keys can silently collapse distinct keys into one, losing data:
```rust
let entries = std::mem::take(map);
for (mut key, mut nested) in entries {
    stripped += strip_nul_chars_from_string(&mut key);
    stripped += strip_postgres_jsonb_nul_chars(&mut nested);
    map.insert(key, nested);
}
```
Two distinct keys that differ only by an embedded NUL character become identical after stripping, and `map.insert` silently overwrites one entry with the other -- data loss with no warning, and potentially flipping which value survives depending on iteration order.

2. Lines 344-362 -- the JSON-depth guard `if depth > MAX_JSON_DEPTH { Err(...) }` with recursion calling `validate_json_structure(v, depth + 1)` starting from depth=0 admits 33 nesting levels when MAX_JSON_DEPTH==32 (strict greater-than rather than greater-or-equal), an off-by-one against the documented "32 levels" limit.

Found via a narrate-through audit of crate/sinex-primitives/src/validation/core.rs, mod.rs, pg_identifier.rs using gpt-5.3-codex-spark (2026-08-11).

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Construct a JSON payload with two keys that differ only by an embedded NUL character, run it through the sanitizer -- observe one key's value silently overwrites the other after NUL-stripping.
2. Construct a JSON structure nested exactly 33 levels deep with MAX_JSON_DEPTH=32 -- observe it passes validation.

The #[derive(SourceRecord)] macro (crate/sinex-macros/src/derive_source_record.rs) validates that a struct-level discriminator field name exists among the struct's fields, but does not verify it's the SAME field the event_dispatch match table was built from -- so a struct with `discriminator = "kind"` and #[event_dispatch(...)] on a DIFFERENT field ("kind_str") passes validation and generates a discriminator lookup on the wrong field.

Relevant code (derive_source_record.rs:163-171, 183-187, 196-199):
```rust
if let Some(ref disc_field) = attrs.discriminator_field {
    let found = field_decls.iter().any(|d| &d.name == disc_field);
    if !found { return Err(...); }
}

let disc_info: Option<(&str, &FieldDecl)> =
    if let Some(ref disc_name) = attrs.discriminator_field {
        dispatch_fields
            .first()
            .map(|fd| (disc_name.as_str(), *fd))
            .or({ None })
    } else { ... };
```

Concrete wrong-codegen scenario: `#[source_record(..., discriminator = "kind")]` on field `kind`, but `#[event_dispatch(...)]` is on a different field `kind_str`. The macro passes validation (kind exists as a field) and generates a discriminator on `kind` with the case table built from `kind_str`'s dispatch attribute. The generated parser then looks up discriminator values from the wrong field at runtime, so events are dispatched or fall back to the unknown-handler incorrectly.

Found via a targeted audit of crate/sinex-macros/src/source_record.rs using gpt-5.3-codex-spark (2026-08-11), verified against the quoted code.

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Define a struct with #[source_record(discriminator = "kind")] where field "kind" exists.
2. Put #[event_dispatch(...)] on a DIFFERENT field, e.g. "kind_str".
3. Compile -- observe it succeeds (validation only checks the discriminator field exists, not that it matches the dispatch field).
4. Trace the generated parser code -- observe the discriminator lookup reads "kind" while the case table was built from "kind_str"'s values, so dispatch is wrong at runtime.

Checkpoint anti-clobber comparison in SourceDriverRuntime::save_state is vacuous/backwards for AdapterBackedSource -- it discards the freshly-computed checkpoint and re-persists a stale one, wasting a KV write on every save and risking re-emission of already-processed material after a hard crash.

crate/sinexd/src/runtime/source_driver.rs:462-510 (SourceDriverRuntime::save_state):
    let mut ckpt_state = CheckpointState { checkpoint: self.state.checkpoint.clone(), processed_count: 0, ... };
    if let Some(cm) = &self.checkpoint_manager {
        let existing_state = cm.load_checkpoint().await?;
        if existing_state.revision > 0 {
            if existing_state.processed_count > ckpt_state.processed_count {
                ckpt_state = existing_state;   // discards the freshly-computed checkpoint wholesale
            }
        }
        self.state.revision = cm.save_checkpoint(&ckpt_state).await?;

ckpt_state.processed_count is hardcoded 0 for every SourceDriver. Meanwhile AdapterBackedSource (crate/sinexd/src/runtime/parser/adapter_source.rs:1808-1815, persist_stream_checkpoint_if_due -- backs sqlite_row/sqlite_snapshot, wired via crate/sinexd/src/sources/source_factory.rs:191-204) writes directly to the SAME NATS-KV checkpoint entry with a real, monotonically growing processed_count. So existing_state.processed_count(N) > 0 is true essentially always, and save_state() throws away the checkpoint it just computed, re-persisting the stale existing KV entry instead -- inverse of the intended "protect fresher checkpoint from clobber" logic.

Currently near-harmless in practice: drain_adapter's own unconditional persist_stream_checkpoint_if_due(state, true) (adapter_source.rs:1756) always fires right before scan_historical/run_continuous return, using the same state object source_driver.rs serializes moments later -- so today the "discarded" value is content-identical, just a wasted KV write+revision bump. But it's fragile: any scan that legitimately makes zero progress (persist early-returns on state.cursor.is_none(), adapter_source.rs:1771-1773) leaves existing_state stale, and a hard crash (not graceful shutdown -- the hot-reload file save at source_driver.rs:475-483 only runs when is_shutdown=true) would load that stale KV checkpoint on restart, re-emitting already-processed material.

Also contradicts AdapterBackedSource::capabilities() declaring manages_own_checkpoints: false (adapter_source.rs:1954) while it demonstrably writes checkpoints itself. No test exercises this cross-writer interaction.

Found via exhaustive regional audit of crate/sinexd/src/runtime/{acquisition_manager,source_driver,sqlite_source,watcher_handle,supervised_watcher,file_tailer,batch_importer,ingestion_helpers,scan_progress}.rs (2026-08-11).

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Run an AdapterBackedSource-backed source (e.g. sqlite_row) that legitimately makes zero progress on a scan (state.cursor.is_none()).
2. Observe save_state's anti-clobber check discards the freshly-computed checkpoint (which has processed_count:0 hardcoded) in favor of the existing KV entry (which has a real, higher processed_count).
3. Hard-kill sinexd (not graceful shutdown) at this point and restart -- observe it loads the stale KV checkpoint, since the hot-reload file save only runs on graceful shutdown.

xtask run.rs's execute_tether() reports success even when the NATS/JetStream streaming session actually terminated due to a real error.

xtask/src/commands/run.rs:1976-2069:
```rust
let stream_handle = tokio::spawn(async move {
    if let Err(e) = session_clone.stream_events(tx).await {
        eprintln!("[tether] Streaming error: {e}");
    }
    session_clone
});
...
Ok(CommandResult::success()
    .with_message(format!("Tether session closed. Received {count} events."))
    .with_data(serde_json::json!({..., "errors": stats.errors(), ...})))
```
stream_events() (xtask/src/sandbox/tether.rs:408+) has no internal cancellation path -- it only ever returns because of a real NATS/consumer error (self.stats.inc_errors(); return Err(...) at tether.rs:461-464); the only other exit is the whole process being SIGINT-killed outright (which wouldn't reach this code at all). So any time this command's own event loop exits mid-session, it's because of a real error, yet execute() always reports CommandResult::success(). Weaker/detectable: stats.errors() is exposed in the JSON data.errors field, but the descriptive message only goes to stderr.

Found via a targeted follow-up audit (Sonnet) hunting for the success-reported-on-failure pattern across sinexctl/xtask (2026-08-11).

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Run xtask run --tether against a NATS session, then trigger a real streaming error (e.g. kill the NATS connection mid-session) rather than a clean user interrupt.
2. Observe CommandResult::success() is still returned with a 'Tether session closed' message, despite stream_events() having exited due to a real error.

start_invocation's error-handling path in xtask's history DB swallows ROLLBACK failures, which can leave the SQLite connection's transaction state inconsistent for subsequent attempts.

crate/xtask/src/history/db/invocations.rs:31-34 and 50-51:
```rust
Err(err) => {
    let _ = self.conn.execute("ROLLBACK", []);
    return Err(color_eyre::eyre::Report::from(err))
        .wrap_err("failed to clear synthetic marker");
}
...
Err(err) => {
    let _ = self.conn.execute("ROLLBACK", []);
    return Err(err.into());
}
```
If the ROLLBACK itself fails (e.g. connection issue, or nothing to roll back due to prior state confusion), the failure is discarded. The function still returns the original error, but the connection may be left in an inconsistent transaction state -- start_invocation can then repeatedly fail on the same connection, silently dropping/losing invocation telemetry.

Found via a targeted audit of crate/xtask/src/history/db/invocations.rs using gpt-5.3-codex-spark (2026-08-11), verified against the quoted code.

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Trigger the error path in start_invocation that leads to a ROLLBACK (e.g. failing the initial insert/marker-clear).
2. Simulate the ROLLBACK itself failing (e.g. connection-level fault injection).
3. Observe the ROLLBACK error is discarded (let _ =) and the function returns only the original error, with no signal that the connection's transaction state may now be inconsistent.

#[event_dispatch("..." => "...")] match-string parsing in the SourceRecord derive macro strips surrounding quote characters from the raw token text instead of decoding it as a proper Rust string literal (LitStr) -- escape sequences are not decoded.

Relevant code (derive_source_record.rs:1040-1045, 1076-1081):
```rust
Some(TokenTree::Literal(lit)) => {
    let s = lit.to_string();
    // Strip surrounding quotes.
    if s.starts_with('"') && s.ends_with('"') {
        s[1..s.len() - 1].to_string()
    } else { ... }
}
```

Concrete wrong-codegen scenario: `#[event_dispatch("line\nbreak" => "evt")]` does not get `\n` decoded as a real newline (or any escape decoded at all) -- the generated case value becomes the literal escaped token text (backslash-n, two characters) rather than the runtime string value the attribute author intended. Discriminator matching then silently fails for any input containing the actual decoded character, even though the attribute looks syntactically valid.

Found via a targeted audit of crate/sinex-macros/src/source_record.rs using gpt-5.3-codex-spark (2026-08-11), verified against the quoted code.

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Define #[event_dispatch("line\\nbreak" => "evt")] on a source record field.
2. Compile and inspect the generated match arm -- observe the case value is the literal two-character sequence backslash-n rather than a decoded newline.
3. Feed a runtime value containing an actual newline -- observe it never matches this case.

crate/sinexd/src/runtime/stage_as_you_go.rs:373-379 (emit_event_with_provenance) falls back to Timestamp::now() only as a last resort when the primary resolution (material_started_at, which traces to the persisted staged_at/BEGIN ledger row and is reproducible) itself returns None -- i.e. an internal acquisition_handles/material_registry lookup inconsistency for a material_id that should exist, not routine malformed source data.

```rust
if event.ts_orig.is_none() {
    event.ts_orig = Some(self.material_started_at(source_material_id).await.unwrap_or_else(Timestamp::now));
}
```

Lower severity than the other ts_orig-fabrication findings in this batch (sinex-10ef, sinex-xfz3, sinex-k42g, sinex-dmz9): the primary path is correct and reproducible, this only fires on an internal-consistency anomaly that shouldn't normally occur. Filed as a residual reproducibility gap for completeness, not urgent.

Found via a targeted follow-up audit (Sonnet) of this session's confirmed activitywatch.rs finding (2026-08-11).

## Acceptance
Decide whether this internal-consistency-anomaly fallback deserves a hard error/log instead of silent Timestamp::now() substitution, given it indicates acquisition_handles/material_registry are out of sync for a material_id that should exist.

