CORRECTION (2026-08-11, operator-prompted re-verification): this bead's original claim is WRONG. Verified against the actual EventBuilder::build() implementation (crate/sinex-primitives/src/events/builder.rs:275-287, comment tagged "#1570 Prong B"):

    // Material-provenance events without an explicit timestamp leave
    // `ts_orig = None` as the "derive me at persistence" signal; the event_engine
    // admission stage resolves it from the source-material timing tier.
    // Derived events have no material to resolve against, so they keep the
    // wall-clock fallback (their synthesis time).
    let ts_orig = match (&provenance, self.timestamp) {
        (_, Some(ts)) => Some(ts),
        (Provenance::Material { .. }, None) => None,
        (Provenance::Derived { .. }, None) => Some(Timestamp::now()),
    };

All four automata originally cited here (document_parser, entity_extractor, entity_resolver, tag_applier) produce DERIVED events. `context.ts_orig.unwrap_or_else(Timestamp::now)` in each of them is NOT a doctrine violation -- it's a manual reimplementation of exactly the policy the builder itself would apply by default if the automaton omitted the timestamp entirely. There is no "never falsify provenance clocks" violation here; that tripwire is about backdating ts_coided/minting UUIDv7 from ts_orig, a completely different mechanism from this designed Derived-event fallback.

Closing this bead as invalid. The one real, narrower finding buried in the same wave128 batch -- crate/sinexd/src/sources/source_contracts/desktop/activitywatch.rs, a MATERIAL-provenance parser that fabricates ts_orig and passes it explicitly, bypassing the None-then-resolve-from-raw.temporal_ledger path material events are specifically designed to use -- is filed separately, not conflated with this incorrect 4-automata claim.
